DESCRIPTIONS
.in +5
.br
Crypt returns a pointer to a string of thirteen printable characters
derived from the password
.bd
pw
and the two-character
.bd
salt.
The same password and salt always give the same string, so a stored
one can be compared against the result of hashing what the user
types.
.sp
This is the hash the password file has always carried here, and it
is not the DES one.  It is the older big-multiply construction: the
salt and the password are folded into a sixteen-byte key, seventeen
rounds of a cyclic three-by-three multiply-accumulate are run over
two twelve-byte blocks, and eleven characters of the result are
filtered into a printable alphabet.  The seed string that gives it
away is "Here is is !?!".
.sp
The salt is the first two characters of the string being compared
against; when a new password is set, two characters are chosen for
it, traditionally from the clock.
.sp
The whole of
.bd
pw
is used.  The loop that folds it walks to the end of the string and
adds each character into the twelve-byte key, wrapping around, so
the thirteenth character lands on the same byte as the first and a
long password is folded rather than truncated.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The answer is in a static buffer that the next call overwrites.
Copy it if it is to be kept.
.sp
Crypt is in libc - lib/libc/crypt.c - and was login's own code until
it was moved there, which is where the 1982 image had it.
.sp
This is not the
.bd
crypt
of
.bd
crypt (1),
which encrypts a file: that is a different routine with a different
name for the same thing.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
